<h1>Cookie &amp; Cracker Packaging Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">Cookie and cracker packaging conveyors handle fragile baked products through counting, stacking, wrapping, tray loading, and case packing without breakage. These lines use slip-torque accumulation, zero-gap belt transfers, counter stackers, and servo-driven wrapper infeed conveyors to maintain product integrity at high production rates. Many packaging machine builders supply integrated cookie and cracker systems with their own conveyor subsystems for fragile product handling.</p>
<h2 id="challenges-heading">Why Cookies &amp; Crackers Break</h2>
<p>Cookies and crackers break from three causes on packaging lines: impact at transfer points, compression during accumulation, and vibration during transport. Each cause requires a different conveyor solution. High breakage wastes raw materials, slows production (jams from broken pieces), and creates customer complaints from damaged product in the package.</p>
<h3>Impact Breakage</h3>
<p>Every transfer point between conveyor sections creates an impact opportunity. Products fall across gaps, hit dead plates, or land on hard roller surfaces. Cookie packaging lines minimize transfers and use small-radius (tapered-nose) belt transfers that overlap the receiving belt or leave only a very small gap. Where transfers cannot be avoided, air-cushion transfer plates use a thin layer of compressed air to float product across the gap.</p>
<h3>Compression Breakage</h3>
<p>When products accumulate (stop) on a running conveyor, back-pressure from upstream product pushes against stopped product. Standard conveyors apply full belt friction force to stopped products, enough to crush cookies. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> with low back-pressure or zero-pressure technology solve this. In Shuttleworth's <a href="https://www.shuttleworth.com/products/slip-torque-technology/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Slip-Torque</a> design, segmented, loose-fit rollers ride on driven stainless steel shafts. When product stops, the rollers beneath it also stop, so accumulated product sees very low back-pressure.</p>
<h3>Vibration Breakage</h3>
<p>High-frequency vibration from belt splices, roller bearings, and drive components cracks thin products like crackers. Solutions include seamless (welded) belts, precision-balanced rollers, and vibration-damped conveyor frames. Running conveyors at consistent speed (avoiding frequent start-stop cycles) also reduces vibration damage.</p>
<h2 id="stacker-heading">Counter Stackers &amp; Slug Formation</h2>
<p>Counter stackers count individual cookies on a high-speed belt, form them into stacks (slugs) of a specified count, and deliver slugs to the wrapping machine at the correct pitch. It is often the most mechanically complex conveyor subsystem in a cookie packaging line.</p>
<p>The counter stacker process works in four steps:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Counting:</strong> Photoeyes or laser sensors count individual products on a high-speed belt. Sensor response must separate closely spaced products at belt speed, so confirm minimum product gap and maximum belt speed with the supplier.</li>
<li><strong>Grouping:</strong> A servo-driven diverter or speed-change belt separates counted groups from the continuous product stream. The gap between groups provides time for the stacking mechanism to reset.</li>
<li><strong>Stacking:</strong> Products drop or slide into a vertical stack. Edge-stacking (products on edge like books) or flat-stacking (products face-up in a pile) depends on the product shape and wrapper format.</li>
<li><strong>Slug delivery:</strong> The completed slug transfers to a wrapper infeed conveyor. Servo timing ensures the slug arrives at the wrapper at the correct position and speed. For more on <a href="/conveyor-functions/stacking-machines/">stacking machine technology</a>, see our dedicated guide.</li>
</ol>
<p>Counter stackers are sold both as part of a wrapper builder's integrated line and as standalone units for lines where different wrappers handle different products. Integrated units simplify controls; standalone units need a defined handshake with the wrapper.</p>
<h2 id="infeed-heading">Wrapper Infeed Conveyors</h2>
<p>The wrapper infeed conveyor is the critical link between the stacking system and the flow wrapper. It must deliver product slugs at exact pitch (center-to-center spacing) and speed to match the wrapper's film feed cycle. Timing errors produce misregistered packages: the seal cuts through the product instead of between slugs.</p>
<h3>Slug-Wrap (Horizontal Stack) Infeed</h3>
<p>Slug-wrap lines deliver horizontal stacks of cookies on-edge to a horizontal form-fill-seal (HFFS) wrapper. The <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed conveyor</a> uses flights (pusher bars) attached to a timing belt to maintain exact slug spacing. Servo-driven flights allow recipe-based pitch changes for different slug lengths.</p>
<h3>Tray-Pack Infeed</h3>
<p>Tray-pack lines load cookies flat into thermoformed or pre-made trays. A <a href="/conveyor-functions/tray-loading/">tray loading conveyor</a> positions empty trays under a pick-and-place mechanism (vacuum cups, mechanical fingers, or delta robots) that places counted cookies into tray cavities. The loaded tray transfers to overwrap or top-seal equipment. Tray loading conveyors use <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">servo indexing</a> to position trays precisely under the loading head.</p>
<h3>Pillow-Pack Infeed</h3>
<p>Pillow-pack (individual wrap) lines wrap each cookie individually in a small film pillow. The infeed conveyor spaces individual cookies at the wrapper pitch. Servo-driven phasing belts accelerate cookies to the correct spacing. This format is common for single-serve cookies in vending, foodservice, and variety packs.</p>
<h2 id="accum-heading">Gentle Accumulation</h2>
<p>Cookie and cracker lines need accumulation between the oven and the packaging equipment. The oven runs continuously; the packaging machine stops for film splices, jam clearing, or changeover. Without accumulation, every packaging stop shuts down the oven, wasting product already in the baking cycle.</p>
<p>Standard <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation conveyors</a> apply too much back-pressure for fragile cookies. Cookie lines use two gentle accumulation approaches:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Slip-torque accumulation:</strong> <a href="https://www.shuttleworth.com/products/slip-torque-technology/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Slip-Torque</a> conveyors use segmented, loose-fit rollers on driven shafts. Rollers under stopped product stop while the shafts keep turning, so stationary product sees very low back-pressure. Multiple zones accumulate product without compression. Zones release in sequence when the downstream machine restarts.</li>
<li><strong>Mass-flow accumulation:</strong> Wide belt conveyors accumulate product in a mass (random orientation) at low depth. Products spread across the full belt width rather than stacking up. A <a href="/conveyor-functions/product-handling/spreader/">spreader conveyor</a> distributes product evenly. This approach suits round cookies that can contact each other without damage but is unsuitable for decorated or enrobed products.</li>
</ul>
<p>Accumulation capacity (minutes of buffer) depends on packaging machine reliability. Size the buffer to cover the longest routine stop you expect, such as a film splice or jam clear, using your own downtime records. Each minute of buffer needs conveyor surface area for one minute of production: buffer area = production rate x buffer minutes x area per product. Plan floor space accordingly.</p>
<h2 id="lane-heading">Multi-Lane Conveyor Layouts</h2>
<p>High-volume cookie and cracker lines use multiple parallel lanes from oven to wrapper. A wide oven belt produces cookies in several lanes simultaneously. Each lane feeds its own stacker and wrapper, or multiple lanes merge into a single high-speed wrapper with a <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating and laning system</a>.</p>
<p>Lane-splitting conveyors use adjustable guide rails or servo-positioned diverters to separate the wide oven output into individual lanes. Lane width adjustment accommodates different cookie diameters. Servo-positioned guides change lane width through recipe selection on the HMI, with no manual adjustment required during changeover.</p>
<p>Lane balancing ensures equal product count across all lanes. Unequal lane loading causes one wrapper to starve while another jams. Photoeye sensors at each lane count products and signal the lane-splitting conveyor to redistribute product toward underloaded lanes.</p>
<h2 id="case-heading">Case Packing &amp; End-of-Line</h2>
<p>Wrapped cookie and cracker packages move from wrappers to <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packing conveyors</a>. The conveyor between wrapper and case packer collates packages into the case pattern. Collating conveyors group packages using timing belts, lane guides, and servo-positioned stops.</p>
<p><a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/robotic-case-packing/">Robotic case packers</a> with delta robots handle the high speed and gentle touch required for cookie packages. Delta robots pick single or multiple packages and place them into cases. The robot vision system compensates for package orientation variations on the infeed conveyor.</p>
<p>After case sealing, case conveyors transport sealed cases to palletizing. Cookie and cracker cases are relatively light and stack well, making them suitable for high-level palletizers. The <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/pallet-dispenser/">pallet dispenser</a> feeds empty pallets to the palletizer, and case conveyors deliver sealed cases at the palletizer's cycle rate.</p>
